21. TESTNG 🔴 — INTERVIEW ANSWERS
371. What is TestNG?
Simple

TestNG is a testing framework for Java inspired by JUnit. It provides annotations, assertions, grouping, parameterization, parallel execution, listeners, reporting, and test dependencies.

Interview answer

“TestNG is a Java-based testing framework that I use with Selenium to organize and execute automation test cases. It provides annotations such as @Test, @BeforeMethod, and @AfterMethod, along with features like DataProvider, grouping, dependencies, parallel execution, assertions, listeners, and reporting. In my automation framework, TestNG acts as the test execution layer.”

372. Why TestNG?

TestNG provides features that are very useful for Selenium automation:

Annotations
Test grouping
Priority
Dependencies
DataProvider
Parallel execution
Assertions
Listeners
Retry mechanism
testng.xml
Reports
Interview answer

“I prefer TestNG because it provides strong support for automation frameworks. For example, I can execute tests in parallel, parameterize tests using DataProvider, group smoke and regression tests, control dependencies, capture failures using listeners, and generate execution reports.”

373. TestNG annotations?

Important annotations:

@BeforeSuite
@BeforeTest
@BeforeClass
@BeforeMethod

@Test

@AfterMethod
@AfterClass
@AfterTest
@AfterSuite

There are also:

@DataProvider
@Parameters
@Listeners
@Factory
374. Annotation execution order?

Typical execution order:

@BeforeSuite
    ↓
@BeforeTest
    ↓
@BeforeClass
    ↓
@BeforeMethod
    ↓
@Test
    ↓
@AfterMethod
    ↓
@AfterClass
    ↓
@AfterTest
    ↓
@AfterSuite

Example:

@BeforeSuite
public void beforeSuite() {}

@BeforeTest
public void beforeTest() {}

@BeforeClass
public void beforeClass() {}

@BeforeMethod
public void beforeMethod() {}

@Test
public void testLogin() {}

@AfterMethod
public void afterMethod() {}

@AfterClass
public void afterClass() {}

@AfterTest
public void afterTest() {}

@AfterSuite
public void afterSuite() {}
Interview tip

Don't simply memorize the order. Explain what each level is used for.

375. What is @BeforeSuite?

Runs once before all tests in the entire suite.

@BeforeSuite
public void setupSuite() {
    System.out.println("Suite setup");
}
Real-time use

For:

Global configuration
Report initialization
Environment setup
Database setup
Interview answer

“I use @BeforeSuite for activities that need to happen only once for the complete test suite, such as initializing reporting or common suite-level configuration.”

376. What is @BeforeTest?

Runs before the <test> tag defined in testng.xml.

@BeforeTest
public void setupTest() {
}

Useful when different <test> sections require different configurations.

377. What is @BeforeClass?

Runs once before the first test method of a class.

@BeforeClass
public void setupClass() {
}
Real-time use

For class-level setup such as:

Creating page objects
Initializing class-level resources
378. What is @BeforeMethod?

Runs before every @Test method.

@BeforeMethod
public void setup() {
    driver = new ChromeDriver();
}

If there are 5 test methods, it normally executes 5 times.

Real-time use

Very commonly used for:

Launch browser
Navigate to application
Login
379. What is @Test?

@Test identifies a method as a TestNG test method.

@Test
public void loginTest() {
    System.out.println("Login test");
}

TestNG discovers and executes this method.

380. What is @AfterMethod?

Runs after every @Test method.

@AfterMethod
public void tearDown() {
    driver.quit();
}
Real-time use

Commonly used for:

Logout
Browser cleanup
Screenshot on failure
Clearing test data
381. What is @AfterClass?

Runs after all test methods in a particular class have completed.

@AfterClass
public void closeClass() {
}
382. What is @AfterTest?

Runs after the <test> section in testng.xml.

@AfterTest
public void afterTest() {
}
383. What is @AfterSuite?

Runs once after the complete TestNG suite finishes.

@AfterSuite
public void afterSuite() {
}
Real-time use

For:

Closing reporting
Sending final execution summary
Cleaning global resources
384. What is priority in TestNG?

priority controls the execution order of test methods.

@Test(priority = 1)
public void login() {}

@Test(priority = 2)
public void searchProduct() {}

@Test(priority = 3)
public void logout() {}

Execution:

login
searchProduct
logout
Important interview point

Don't make tests unnecessarily dependent on priority.

“I use priority only when execution order is genuinely required. I generally prefer independent tests because independent tests are easier to execute in parallel and maintain.”

385. What is dependsOnMethods?

It makes one test dependent on another test.

@Test
public void login() {
}

@Test(dependsOnMethods = "login")
public void searchProduct() {
}

If login() fails, searchProduct() is skipped.

Interview answer

“I use dependsOnMethods when there is a genuine business dependency between tests. However, I avoid unnecessary dependencies because they can cause cascading skips and reduce parallel execution.”

386. What is dependsOnGroups?

A test can depend on an entire group.

@Test(groups = "login")
public void login() {
}

@Test(dependsOnGroups = "login")
public void checkout() {
}

The checkout test executes only after the required group has successfully completed.

387. What are groups?

Groups allow us to categorize tests.

@Test(groups = "smoke")
public void loginTest() {}

@Test(groups = "regression")
public void searchTest() {}

@Test(groups = "regression")
public void paymentTest() {}

Then execute selected groups through testng.xml.

<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="smoke"/>
    </run>
</groups>
Real-time groups

Typical groups:

smoke
sanity
regression
integration
api
ui
critical
388. What is DataProvider?

@DataProvider is used for data-driven testing.

Example:

@DataProvider(name = "loginData")
public Object[][] getLoginData() {
    return new Object[][] {
        {"user1", "pass1"},
        {"user2", "pass2"},
        {"user3", "pass3"}
    };
}

@Test(dataProvider = "loginData")
public void loginTest(String username, String password) {
    System.out.println(username + " " + password);
}

The test executes three times.

Real-time use

Instead of creating:

loginTestUser1()
loginTestUser2()
loginTestUser3()

we create one test and provide multiple datasets.

389. What are parameters in TestNG?

@Parameters allows values to be passed from testng.xml.

testng.xml
<parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>

Java:

@Parameters("browser")
@BeforeMethod
public void setup(String browser) {

    if (browser.equals("chrome")) {
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
    }
}
DataProvider vs Parameters
DataProvider	Parameters
Multiple test data	Configuration values
Method-level data	Usually XML-level values
Supports multiple datasets	Usually predefined parameter
Good for data-driven testing	Good for browser/environment configuration
Strong interview answer

“I use @Parameters mainly for configuration values such as browser or environment, whereas I use @DataProvider when I need to execute the same test with multiple sets of test data.”

390. What is invocationCount?

It specifies how many times a test should execute.

@Test(invocationCount = 5)
public void testSearch() {
}

The test executes 5 times.

Can also combine with parallel:

@Test(
    invocationCount = 10,
    threadPoolSize = 3
)
public void testSearch() {
}
391. What is timeOut?

It defines the maximum time allowed for a test.

@Test(timeOut = 5000)
public void testLogin() {
}

If execution exceeds the specified timeout, TestNG marks the test as failed.

Important

This is different from Selenium's explicit wait.

TestNG timeout controls test execution time; Selenium explicit wait waits for a specific condition.

392. What is expectedExceptions?

Used when a test is expected to throw an exception.

@Test(expectedExceptions = ArithmeticException.class)
public void divisionTest() {
    int result = 10 / 0;
}

If ArithmeticException occurs → test passes.

If no exception occurs → test fails.

393. What is enabled=false?

It disables a test.

@Test(enabled = false)
public void oldTest() {
}

TestNG will not execute it.

Interview tip

Don't use this as a permanent solution for broken tests.

“I use enabled=false temporarily when a test should not participate in execution, but I would normally track the reason through defect or test maintenance rather than permanently disabling tests.”

394. What are assertions?

Assertions validate expected vs actual results.

Example:

Assert.assertEquals(actualTitle, expectedTitle);

If assertion passes → test continues.

If assertion fails → test fails.

Common assertions:

Assert.assertEquals()
Assert.assertNotEquals()
Assert.assertTrue()
Assert.assertFalse()
Assert.assertNull()
Assert.assertNotNull()
395. What is Hard Assertion?

Hard assertion immediately stops the current test method when the assertion fails.

Assert.assertEquals(actual, expected);

System.out.println("This may not execute");

If assertion fails, subsequent statements in that test method won't execute.

Real-time use

Use hard assertion for critical validations.

Example:

Assert.assertTrue(loginPage.isDisplayed());

If login page isn't displayed, continuing the test doesn't make sense.

396. What is Soft Assertion?

Soft assertions allow multiple validations to execute before reporting failures.

SoftAssert softAssert = new SoftAssert();

softAssert.assertEquals(actualTitle, expectedTitle);
softAssert.assertTrue(isLogoDisplayed);
softAssert.assertEquals(actualUser, expectedUser);

softAssert.assertAll();
VERY IMPORTANT

Always call:

softAssert.assertAll();

Otherwise failures may not be reported correctly.

Hard vs Soft
Hard	Soft
Stops after failure	Continues
Immediate failure	Failure reported at assertAll()
Critical validation	Multiple independent validations
397. What are TestNG listeners?

Listeners allow us to listen to TestNG execution events and perform custom actions.

Examples:

Test started
Test passed
Test failed
Test skipped
Suite started
Suite finished
Real-time use

I commonly use listeners for:

Screenshot capture
Logging
Reporting
Failure analysis
Retry handling
398. What is ITestListener?

ITestListener is a TestNG listener interface.

Important methods:

onTestStart()
onTestSuccess()
onTestFailure()
onTestSkipped()

Example:

public class TestListener implements ITestListener {

    @Override
    public void onTestFailure(ITestResult result) {
        System.out.println("Test failed: "
                + result.getName());
    }
}
Real-time screenshot example
@Override
public void onTestFailure(ITestResult result) {

    // capture screenshot
    // attach screenshot to report
    // log failure details
}
Strong interview answer

“I use ITestListener for cross-cutting activities such as screenshot capture, logging and reporting. This avoids duplicating failure-handling code inside every test case.”

399. What is Retry Analyzer?

Retry Analyzer allows a failed test to be executed again automatically.

Example:

public class RetryAnalyzer
        implements IRetryAnalyzer {

    private int count = 0;
    private int maxRetry = 2;

    @Override
    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {

        if (count < maxRetry) {
            count++;
            return true;
        }

        return false;
    }
}

Then:

@Test(retryAnalyzer = RetryAnalyzer.class)
public void paymentTest() {
}

If it fails:

1st attempt → Fail
2nd attempt → Retry
3rd attempt → Retry
Important senior-level point

Don't blindly retry every failure.

“I use retry mainly for known transient failures such as temporary environment or network instability. I don't use retry to hide genuine application defects.”

400. What is testng.xml?

testng.xml is a configuration file used to control TestNG execution.

It can define:

Test classes
Groups
Parameters
Parallel execution
Thread count
Included/excluded tests

Example:

<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM
    "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">

<suite name="RegressionSuite">

    <test name="LoginTests">

        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
            <class name="tests.SearchTest"/>
        </classes>

    </test>

</suite>
401. What is parallel execution?

Parallel execution means executing multiple tests at the same time using multiple threads.

Instead of:

Test 1
 ↓
Test 2
 ↓
Test 3

we can execute:

Thread 1 → Test 1
Thread 2 → Test 2
Thread 3 → Test 3

This reduces execution time.

Real-time example

Suppose 300 regression tests take 3 hours sequentially.

With proper parallelization, execution time may be significantly reduced.

But the exact reduction depends on:

Number of tests
Thread count
Environment capacity
Browser/grid capacity
Test dependencies
402. What is thread-count?

thread-count specifies the maximum number of threads used for parallel execution.

<suite name="Regression"
       parallel="methods"
       thread-count="5">

This allows up to 5 parallel execution threads.

Important

Thread count should not simply be set extremely high.

“I choose thread count based on infrastructure capacity, Selenium Grid or cloud capacity, application stability and test independence.”

403. What is parallel="methods"?

It executes test methods in parallel.

<suite name="Suite"
       parallel="methods"
       thread-count="5">

Example:

Thread 1 → testLogin()
Thread 2 → testSearch()
Thread 3 → testPayment()
404. What is parallel="classes"?

Different test classes execute in parallel.

<suite name="Suite"
       parallel="classes"
       thread-count="3">

Example:

Thread 1 → LoginTest
Thread 2 → SearchTest
Thread 3 → PaymentTest

Methods within a class generally remain sequential relative to that class.

405. What is parallel="tests"?

Different <test> tags in testng.xml execute in parallel.

Example:

<suite name="Suite"
       parallel="tests"
       thread-count="3">

    <test name="ChromeTests">
        ...
    </test>

    <test name="FirefoxTests">
        ...
    </test>

</suite>

This is useful when you want separate test configurations to run concurrently.

406. What is DataProvider parallel?

By default, DataProvider executions can be sequential.

We can enable parallel execution:

@DataProvider(
    name = "loginData",
    parallel = true
)
public Object[][] getData() {

    return new Object[][] {
        {"user1", "pass1"},
        {"user2", "pass2"},
        {"user3", "pass3"},
        {"user4", "pass4"}
    };
}

Now multiple datasets can execute concurrently.

Critical Selenium interview point

When running Selenium tests in parallel, do not share one WebDriver instance across threads.

Use ThreadLocal<WebDriver> or another thread-safe driver management approach.

407. How do you rerun failed tests?

There are several approaches.

Approach 1 — TestNG failed-test XML

After execution, TestNG generates:

test-output/testng-failed.xml

This can be used to rerun failed tests.

Approach 2 — Retry Analyzer
@Test(retryAnalyzer = RetryAnalyzer.class)
public void testPayment() {
}

This retries during execution.

Approach 3 — CI pipeline

In CI/CD, failed tests can be collected and rerun through the pipeline.

Strong interview answer

“For transient failures, I can use Retry Analyzer with a limited retry count. For analyzing the failed suite, TestNG also generates testng-failed.xml, which can be used to rerun failed tests. In CI, I additionally configure the pipeline to handle failed-test reruns where appropriate.”

408. How do you generate reports?

TestNG itself generates basic execution reports.

Typically:

test-output/

contains TestNG results.

In a real framework, I usually integrate a reporting solution such as:

Allure
ExtentReports

depending on project requirements.

Typical reporting flow
TestNG
   ↓
ITestListener
   ↓
Capture failure
   ↓
Screenshot
   ↓
Logs
   ↓
Test result
   ↓
Allure / Extent Report
Strong 5-year SDET answer

“In my framework, TestNG manages test execution and listeners capture execution events. On failure, the listener captures the exception, screenshot and relevant logs and attaches them to the report. We integrate a reporting solution such as Allure or ExtentReports so that the team can see passed, failed and skipped tests along with failure details. The reports are also published as CI/CD build artifacts.”

